1
00:00:00,182 --> 00:00:03,734
Every SAT math question
lets you use Desmos,

2
00:00:03,754 --> 00:00:06,413
the graphing calculator
built right into the test.

3
00:00:06,433 --> 00:00:09,233
A lot of students only
use it for arithmetic.

4
00:00:09,253 --> 00:00:11,790
That leaves a lot of
easy points on the table.

5
00:00:11,848 --> 00:00:16,328
Here are eleven ways to use
it on real SAT question types,

6
00:00:16,348 --> 00:00:18,848
starting basic and getting more clever.

7
00:00:19,026 --> 00:00:21,884
Each one comes with a
practice question you can try.

8
00:00:21,929 --> 00:00:24,623
And at the end, I'll show
you when not to use Desmos,

9
00:00:24,643 --> 00:00:27,250
because sometimes it's slower
than just doing the math.

10
00:00:27,302 --> 00:00:28,175
Trick one.

11
00:00:28,195 --> 00:00:30,461
Solve any equation by graphing.

12
00:00:30,481 --> 00:00:35,568
Here's a question: What value of x
satisfies three times x minus four,

13
00:00:35,588 --> 00:00:38,640
plus seven, equals two x plus nine?

14
00:00:38,660 --> 00:00:42,337
Pause and try it, with or without Desmos.

15
00:00:45,593 --> 00:00:51,073
In Desmos, type the left side as
y equals three times x minus four,

16
00:00:51,093 --> 00:00:52,164
plus seven.

17
00:00:52,307 --> 00:00:56,180
Then type the right side as
y equals two x plus nine.

18
00:00:56,200 --> 00:00:57,216
Two lines.

19
00:00:57,236 --> 00:00:59,449
The answer is wherever they cross.

20
00:00:59,879 --> 00:01:03,180
You'll need to zoom out to see it,
because they cross pretty far from the

21
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:03,836
middle.

22
00:01:03,856 --> 00:01:07,192
Click the intersection, and
Desmos shows the point fourteen,

23
00:01:07,212 --> 00:01:08,086
thirty-seven.

24
00:01:08,106 --> 00:01:10,050
The x value is the answer.

25
00:01:10,070 --> 00:01:11,806
x equals fourteen.

26
00:01:11,826 --> 00:01:14,769
You can also type the whole
equation exactly as written,

27
00:01:14,789 --> 00:01:18,163
and Desmos draws a vertical
line at the solution.

28
00:01:18,183 --> 00:01:22,039
Either way, you never had to
distribute or move a single term.

29
00:01:22,076 --> 00:01:23,076
Trick two.

30
00:01:23,219 --> 00:01:24,914
Systems of equations.

31
00:01:24,934 --> 00:01:27,735
If x and y is the solution to the system,

32
00:01:27,755 --> 00:01:32,684
two x plus three y equals
twelve, and x minus y equals one,

33
00:01:32,755 --> 00:01:34,934
what is x plus y?

34
00:01:34,967 --> 00:01:36,253
Pause and try it.

35
00:01:39,458 --> 00:01:43,116
Type both equations into Desmos
exactly as they're written.

36
00:01:43,136 --> 00:01:45,366
You don't need to solve for y first.

37
00:01:45,386 --> 00:01:48,205
Desmos handles that form just fine.

38
00:01:48,225 --> 00:01:51,276
Click where they cross: three, two.

39
00:01:51,296 --> 00:01:54,312
So x is three and y is two.

40
00:01:54,332 --> 00:01:55,669
But read the question again.

41
00:01:55,689 --> 00:01:57,418
It asks for x plus y.

42
00:01:57,438 --> 00:02:00,169
The answer is five, not three.

43
00:02:00,189 --> 00:02:01,455
Trick three.

44
00:02:01,475 --> 00:02:02,955
Counting solutions.

45
00:02:02,975 --> 00:02:06,082
How many solutions does this system have?

46
00:02:06,439 --> 00:02:09,668
y equals x squared minus four x plus one,

47
00:02:09,688 --> 00:02:12,761
and y equals negative two x plus one.

48
00:02:12,781 --> 00:02:14,067
Pause and try it.

49
00:02:17,271 --> 00:02:18,501
Graph both.

50
00:02:18,521 --> 00:02:21,466
Every place the graphs
cross is one solution.

51
00:02:21,486 --> 00:02:24,716
Here, the line cuts through
the parabola in two places.

52
00:02:24,736 --> 00:02:26,073
So there are two solutions.

53
00:02:26,093 --> 00:02:27,342
The answer is C.

54
00:02:27,368 --> 00:02:28,598
Same idea for lines.

55
00:02:28,618 --> 00:02:30,811
If two lines cross once, one solution.

56
00:02:30,831 --> 00:02:32,812
If they're parallel, no solution.

57
00:02:32,832 --> 00:02:34,918
If they land right on top of each other,

58
00:02:34,938 --> 00:02:36,224
infinitely many.

59
00:02:36,246 --> 00:02:37,298
Trick four.

60
00:02:37,318 --> 00:02:40,012
X-intercepts, also called zeros, roots,

61
00:02:40,032 --> 00:02:41,155
or solutions.

62
00:02:41,175 --> 00:02:45,691
What is the sum of the solutions to x
squared minus seven x plus ten equals

63
00:02:45,711 --> 00:02:46,452
zero?

64
00:02:46,472 --> 00:02:47,758
Pause and try it.

65
00:02:50,963 --> 00:02:55,014
Graph y equals x squared
minus seven x plus ten,

66
00:02:55,034 --> 00:02:56,820
and click the curve.

67
00:02:56,891 --> 00:02:59,586
Desmos marks where it crosses the x axis:

68
00:02:59,606 --> 00:03:01,157
two and five.

69
00:03:01,177 --> 00:03:03,661
Add them up, and the answer is seven.

70
00:03:03,681 --> 00:03:06,447
Whenever a question says zeros, roots,

71
00:03:06,467 --> 00:03:09,268
x-intercepts, or solutions of a quadratic,

72
00:03:09,288 --> 00:03:12,967
they all mean the same thing:
where the graph hits the x axis.

73
00:03:13,101 --> 00:03:14,117
Trick five.

74
00:03:14,137 --> 00:03:16,137
Maximums and minimums.

75
00:03:16,280 --> 00:03:19,545
The height of a ball, in feet,
t seconds after it's launched,

76
00:03:19,565 --> 00:03:25,010
is h of t equals negative sixteen t
squared plus sixty-four t plus five.

77
00:03:25,030 --> 00:03:26,422
What's the maximum height?

78
00:03:26,473 --> 00:03:27,759
Pause and try it.

79
00:03:30,963 --> 00:03:35,142
Desmos graphs with x and y, so
type it with x instead of t:

80
00:03:35,178 --> 00:03:40,479
y equals negative sixteen x squared
plus sixty-four x plus five.

81
00:03:40,499 --> 00:03:42,729
Zoom out, because the graph goes way up.

82
00:03:42,749 --> 00:03:44,692
Then click the top of the curve.

83
00:03:44,712 --> 00:03:47,213
Desmos shows two, sixty-nine.

84
00:03:47,325 --> 00:03:48,467
Now be careful.

85
00:03:48,539 --> 00:03:50,768
Two is the time when the ball is highest.

86
00:03:50,788 --> 00:03:52,575
Sixty-nine is the height.

87
00:03:52,717 --> 00:03:56,396
The question asks for height,
so the answer is sixty-nine.

88
00:03:56,479 --> 00:03:57,622
Trick six.

89
00:03:57,729 --> 00:03:59,015
Sliders.

90
00:03:59,337 --> 00:04:03,158
This one handles those questions
with a mystery constant.

91
00:04:03,408 --> 00:04:06,674
In the system k x plus three y equals six,

92
00:04:06,694 --> 00:04:10,423
and two x plus y equals
five, k is a constant.

93
00:04:10,443 --> 00:04:14,051
If the system has no solution, what is k?

94
00:04:14,113 --> 00:04:15,398
Pause and try it.

95
00:04:18,603 --> 00:04:21,139
Type both equations into Desmos.

96
00:04:21,353 --> 00:04:25,655
Because k isn't defined, Desmos
offers to add a slider for it.

97
00:04:25,675 --> 00:04:26,353
Click it.

98
00:04:26,373 --> 00:04:29,174
No solution means the lines
are parallel and never cross.

99
00:04:29,194 --> 00:04:32,480
So drag k until the lines are parallel.

100
00:04:32,516 --> 00:04:35,388
Tip: set the slider to
move in steps of one,

101
00:04:35,408 --> 00:04:37,066
so it lands on whole numbers.

102
00:04:37,086 --> 00:04:39,944
The lines go parallel at k equals six.

103
00:04:40,003 --> 00:04:45,126
To double check, the slope of two x
plus y equals five is negative two.

104
00:04:45,146 --> 00:04:50,768
The slope of k x plus three y
equals six is negative k over three.

105
00:04:50,788 --> 00:04:53,468
Set them equal, and k is six.

106
00:04:54,111 --> 00:04:56,769
Sliders also work for questions like,

107
00:04:56,789 --> 00:05:02,182
for what value of c does this parabola
touch the x axis exactly once?

108
00:05:02,205 --> 00:05:03,293
Trick seven.

109
00:05:03,313 --> 00:05:04,562
Inequalities.

110
00:05:04,633 --> 00:05:10,577
Which point is a solution to the system
y is greater than two x minus three,

111
00:05:10,597 --> 00:05:14,598
and y is less than or equal
to negative x plus six?

112
00:05:14,650 --> 00:05:15,936
Pause and try it.

113
00:05:19,140 --> 00:05:20,997
Type both inequalities.

114
00:05:21,355 --> 00:05:26,426
Desmos shades each one, and the solutions
are wherever the shading overlaps.

115
00:05:26,497 --> 00:05:30,191
The dashed line means
greater than, not equal to,

116
00:05:30,211 --> 00:05:32,250
so points on that line don't count.

117
00:05:32,270 --> 00:05:34,699
Now type the four points from the choices.

118
00:05:34,769 --> 00:05:38,000
Only one lands inside the overlap: one,

119
00:05:38,020 --> 00:05:38,643
two.

120
00:05:38,663 --> 00:05:40,208
The answer is C.

121
00:05:40,228 --> 00:05:41,371
Trick eight.

122
00:05:41,478 --> 00:05:42,548
Regression.

123
00:05:42,655 --> 00:05:45,851
This is the most underrated Desmos move.

124
00:05:45,871 --> 00:05:48,851
The table shows three points
from a linear relationship:

125
00:05:48,871 --> 00:05:53,585
one and seven, three and
thirteen, six and twenty-two.

126
00:05:53,728 --> 00:05:55,337
Which equation represents it?

127
00:05:55,357 --> 00:05:56,643
Pause and try it.

128
00:05:59,848 --> 00:06:03,025
In Desmos, add a table
and type the points in.

129
00:06:03,168 --> 00:06:06,970
Then, on a new line, type y
one, then the tilde symbol,

130
00:06:06,990 --> 00:06:10,418
the little squiggle, then m x one plus b.

131
00:06:10,955 --> 00:06:13,704
Desmos finds the line
that fits the points.

132
00:06:13,727 --> 00:06:16,691
It gives m equals three and b equals four.

133
00:06:16,762 --> 00:06:19,493
So the answer is y
equals three x plus four,

134
00:06:19,513 --> 00:06:20,528
choice A.

135
00:06:20,548 --> 00:06:24,243
Notice B and C both work for the
first point but not the others.

136
00:06:24,263 --> 00:06:27,232
That's exactly the trap
regression protects you from.

137
00:06:27,252 --> 00:06:32,982
For exponential tables, use y
one tilde a times b to the x one.

138
00:06:33,002 --> 00:06:34,074
Same idea.

139
00:06:34,097 --> 00:06:34,863
Trick nine.

140
00:06:34,883 --> 00:06:36,327
Equivalent expressions.

141
00:06:36,347 --> 00:06:38,934
Which expression is
equivalent to x plus three,

142
00:06:38,954 --> 00:06:41,811
squared, minus two times x plus three?

143
00:06:41,888 --> 00:06:43,173
Pause and try it.

144
00:06:46,378 --> 00:06:49,037
Graph the original expression as y equals.

145
00:06:49,057 --> 00:06:51,664
Then graph the choices one at a time.

146
00:06:51,735 --> 00:06:54,393
Equivalent expressions
make the exact same graph,

147
00:06:54,413 --> 00:06:57,664
so the right answer will sit
perfectly on top of the original.

148
00:06:57,690 --> 00:06:59,812
Choice A lands right on top.

149
00:06:59,832 --> 00:07:03,891
The answer is x squared
plus four x plus three.

150
00:07:03,911 --> 00:07:04,911
Trick ten.

151
00:07:04,982 --> 00:07:06,089
Functions.

152
00:07:06,268 --> 00:07:09,962
If f of x equals two
x squared minus three,

153
00:07:09,982 --> 00:07:15,374
and g of x equals x plus four,
what is f of g of negative one?

154
00:07:15,432 --> 00:07:16,718
Pause and try it.

155
00:07:19,922 --> 00:07:23,315
Desmos understands function notation.

156
00:07:23,494 --> 00:07:27,652
Type f of x equals two
x squared minus three.

157
00:07:27,672 --> 00:07:30,687
Type g of x equals x plus four.

158
00:07:30,707 --> 00:07:35,438
Then type f of g of negative
one, and Desmos just tells you:

159
00:07:35,458 --> 00:07:36,357
fifteen.

160
00:07:36,377 --> 00:07:41,536
By hand, g of negative one is three,
and f of three is eighteen minus three,

161
00:07:41,556 --> 00:07:42,948
which is fifteen.

162
00:07:43,163 --> 00:07:47,834
Same answer, but Desmos makes it
nearly impossible to slip on the signs.

163
00:07:47,854 --> 00:07:48,854
Trick eleven.

164
00:07:48,961 --> 00:07:50,210
Statistics.

165
00:07:50,424 --> 00:07:53,977
For the data set four, eight, eight, ten,

166
00:07:53,997 --> 00:07:58,067
fifteen, twenty-one, what is
the mean minus the median?

167
00:07:58,101 --> 00:07:59,386
Pause and try it.

168
00:08:02,591 --> 00:08:07,142
Type L equals, then the
numbers inside square brackets.

169
00:08:07,162 --> 00:08:08,964
Now L is a list.

170
00:08:08,984 --> 00:08:13,937
Type mean of L minus median of
L, and Desmos gives you two.

171
00:08:13,957 --> 00:08:17,135
The mean is eleven and the median is nine.

172
00:08:17,172 --> 00:08:20,723
Desmos also has functions for
things like standard deviation,

173
00:08:20,743 --> 00:08:25,135
but on the SAT you'll usually just compare
spreads instead of calculating them.

174
00:08:25,173 --> 00:08:27,938
Now, when shouldn't you use Desmos?

175
00:08:27,958 --> 00:08:29,938
First, word problems.

176
00:08:29,958 --> 00:08:31,565
Desmos can't read.

177
00:08:31,673 --> 00:08:34,010
You still have to turn the
words into an equation,

178
00:08:34,030 --> 00:08:35,666
and that's usually the hard part.

179
00:08:35,686 --> 00:08:38,650
Second, quick arithmetic
and one-step algebra.

180
00:08:38,757 --> 00:08:41,773
If you can do it in your
head in five seconds,

181
00:08:41,793 --> 00:08:43,864
opening Desmos is slower.

182
00:08:43,899 --> 00:08:46,165
Third, watch the window.

183
00:08:46,185 --> 00:08:49,450
If you don't see an
intersection or a vertex,

184
00:08:49,470 --> 00:08:51,862
zoom out before you decide it isn't there.

185
00:08:51,882 --> 00:08:53,346
And one bonus tip.

186
00:08:53,382 --> 00:08:56,898
If the answer choices have
square roots or pi in them,

187
00:08:56,918 --> 00:09:00,362
type each choice into Desmos
to turn it into a decimal,

188
00:09:00,382 --> 00:09:02,418
then compare it to your answer.

189
00:09:02,443 --> 00:09:03,137
Quick recap.

190
00:09:03,157 --> 00:09:05,029
Solve equations by graphing.

191
00:09:05,049 --> 00:09:06,923
Click intersections for systems.

192
00:09:06,943 --> 00:09:08,101
Count crossings.

193
00:09:08,121 --> 00:09:09,922
Click intercepts and vertices.

194
00:09:09,942 --> 00:09:12,173
Use sliders for mystery constants.

195
00:09:12,193 --> 00:09:14,280
Test points against inequalities.

196
00:09:14,300 --> 00:09:16,029
Run regressions on tables.

197
00:09:16,049 --> 00:09:18,441
Graph expressions to check
if they're equivalent.

198
00:09:18,461 --> 00:09:19,834
Evaluate functions.

199
00:09:19,854 --> 00:09:22,460
And use lists for statistics.

200
00:09:22,480 --> 00:09:26,103
The best way to get fast is to practice
with the same calculator you'll use on

201
00:09:26,123 --> 00:09:27,123
test day.

202
00:09:27,266 --> 00:09:29,477
Bluebook's practice
tests have it built in.

203
00:09:29,497 --> 00:09:32,404
Every practice question here
was written for this video,

204
00:09:32,424 --> 00:09:34,869
in the style of the real test.

205
00:09:34,889 --> 00:09:36,761
If this helped, subscribe.

206
00:09:36,781 --> 00:09:42,834
The next video is ten hard SAT math
questions to try before the November seventh

207
00:09:42,854 --> 00:09:43,727
test.

208
00:09:43,747 --> 00:09:44,747
Good luck.
